::SPEECH_U06_L07_GP_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which portfolio artifact best shows speaking organization?
{
~A. A random image with no explanation.#This does not show speaking organization. A portfolio artifact should provide evidence of a communication skill.
=B. A speaking outline with purpose, audience, main points, and transitions.#Correct. This artifact shows how the speaker planned a clear spoken message for an audience.
~C. A list of favorite topics with no speech plan.#This may show interests, but it does not prove speaking organization or readiness.
~D. A blank slide deck template.#A template alone does not show communication skill. The artifact must include meaningful planning.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_GP_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.01
Question:
Which reflection best explains why a slide deck belongs in a communication portfolio?
{
~A. I included it because it has colors.#This focuses on decoration, not communication skill. Reflection should explain what the artifact proves.
~B. I included it because it took a long time.#Effort matters, but a portfolio reflection should identify the skill demonstrated by the artifact.
~C. I included it because every portfolio needs slides.#This is generic and does not explain the specific communication value.
=D. I included it because it shows how I matched visuals to spoken points for a specific audience.#Correct. This reflection connects the artifact to presentation planning and audience awareness.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_GP_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.01
Question:
Which artifact best shows research-supported communication?
{
=A. Briefing notes that include a research question, source evidence, and explanation.#Correct. This artifact shows how research was selected and explained for a communication purpose.
~B. A personal opinion paragraph with no source support.#This does not show research-supported communication because it lacks evidence from a source.
~C. A decorative title page for the portfolio.#A title page can organize the portfolio, but it does not show research skill by itself.
~D. A list of websites with no explanation.#A list alone does not show how sources support a message. The artifact needs source explanation.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_GP_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.DIG.01
Question:
Which portfolio explanation best shows digital communication skill?
{
~A. This flyer looks nice, so it is useful.#This is too vague. Digital communication should be explained through audience, purpose, format, and clarity.
=B. This digital flyer uses headings, readable contrast, and a clear sign-up step for the audience.#Correct. This explanation names specific design choices and connects them to audience use.
~C. This file is digital, so it automatically shows skill.#Being digital is not enough. The artifact must show audience-aware communication choices.
~D. This announcement has many words, so it is complete.#More words do not guarantee clarity. Digital publication should be organized for the audience.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L07_GP_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L07_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.03
Question:
What should a portfolio introduction do for the reviewer?
{
~A. Hide the purpose so the reviewer discovers it later.#This makes the portfolio harder to understand. The introduction should orient the audience.
~B. List files without explaining their communication value.#A list alone does not show mastery. The reviewer needs to know why artifacts were selected.
=C. Explain the portfolio purpose, audience, and skills the reviewer should notice.#Correct. This prepares the reviewer to understand how the artifacts show communication growth and readiness.
~D. Focus only on how difficult the work felt.#Reflection can mention growth, but the introduction should focus on purpose, audience, and demonstrated skills.
}
